rom above and is called the Weyl\nL154@P3: function corresponding to the boundary triplet Π, cf. Section 2.2.\nL155@P3: Since A is assumed to be a symmetric operator with finite deficiency indices\nL156@P3: the pair {AΘ, A0}, where Θ is an arbitrary selfadjoint relation in H, is a com\u0002plete scattering system with a corresponding scattering matrix {SΘ(λ)}. Our\nL157@P3: main result is Theorem 3.8, which states that the direct integral L\nL158@P3: 2\nL159@P3: (R, µL, Hλ)\nL160@P3: performs a spectral representation of the absolutely continuous part Aac\nL161@P3: 0\nL162@P3: of A0\nL163@P3: such that the scattering matrix {SΘ(λ)} of the scattering system {AΘ, A0} has\nL164@P3: the form\nL165@P3: SΘ(λ) = IHλ + 2i\nL166@P3: p\nL167@P3: ℑm (M(λ))Θ − M(λ)\nL168@P3: \u0001−1p\nL169@P3: ℑm (M(λ)) (1.5)\nL170@P3: for a.e. λ ∈ R, where M(λ) := M(λ + i0), µL is the Lebesgue measure and\nL171@P3: Hλ := ran (ℑm (M(λ))). If the Weyl function scalar, i.e. the deficiency indices of\nL172@P3: A are (1, 1), then we immediately restore (1.3) from (1.5), see also Corollary 3.10.\nL173@P3: We note that in [1] (see also [4]) V.M. Adamyan and B.S. Pavlov have already\nL174@P3: obtained a different (unitarily equivalent) expression for the scattering matrix\nL175@P3: of a pair of selfadjoint extensions of a symmetric operator with finite deficiency\nL176@P3: indices.\nL177@P3: We emphasize that the representation (1.5) in terms of the Weyl function\nL178@P3: of a fixed boundary triplet has several advantages, e.g. for Sturm-Liouville\nL179@P3: operators with matrix potentials, Schr¨odinger operators with point interactions\nL180@P3: and Dirac operators the high energy asymptotic of the scattering matrices can\nL181@P3: be calculated and explicit formulas can be given (see Section 5). Furthermore,\nL182@P3: since the difference of the resolvents of AΘ and A0 is a finite rank operator, the\nL183@P3: complete scattering system {AΘ, A0} admits a so-called spectral shift function\nL184@P3: ξΘ, cf. [27] and e.g. [9, 10]. Recall that ξΘ is a real function summable with\nL185@P3: weight (1 + λ\nL186@P3: 2\nL187@P3: )\nL188@P3: −1\nL189@P3: such that the trace formula\nL190@P3: tr (AΘ − z)\nL191@P3: −1 − (A0 − z)−1\nL192@P3: \u0001\nL193@P3: = −\nL194@P3: Z\nL195@P3: R\nL196@P3: 1\nL197@P3: (λ − z)\nL198@P3: 2\nL199@P3: ξΘ(λ) dλ\nL200@P3: is valid for z ∈ C\\R. The spectral shift function is determined by the trace\nL201@P3: formula up to a real constant. Under the assumption that Θ is a selfadjoint\nL202@P3: matrix, we show that the spectral shift function of {AΘ, A0} is given (up to a\nL203@P3: real constant) by\nL204@P3: ξΘ(λ) = 1\nL205@P3: π\nL206@P3: ℑm\nL207@P3: \nL208@P3: tr (log(M(λ + i0) − Θ))\u0001for a.e. λ ∈ R, (1.6)\nL209@P3: see Theorem 4.1 and [28] for the case n = 1. With this choice of ξΘ and the\nL210@P3: representation (1.5) of the scattering matrix {SΘ(λ)} it is easy to prove an\nL211@P3-4: 4analogue of the Birman-Krein formula (see [8])\nL212@P4: det(SΘ(λ)) = exp−2πiξΘ(λ)\nL213@P4: \u0001\nL214@P4: for a.e. λ ∈ R\nL215@P4: for scattering systems {AΘ, A0} consisting of selfadjoint extensions of a sym\u0002metric operator with finite deficiency indices. Finally we mention that with the\nL216@P4: help of the representation (1.5) in a forthcoming paper the classical Lax-Phillips\nL217@P4: scattering theory will be extended and newly interpreted.\nL218@P4: The paper is organized as follows. In Section 2 we briefly recall the notion\nL219@P4: of boundary triplets and associated Weyl functions and review some standard\nL220@P4: facts. Section 3 is devoted to the study of scattering systems {AΘ, A0} con\u0002sisting of selfadjoint operators which are extension of a densely defined closed\nL221@P4: simple symmetric operator A with finite deficiency indices. After some prepa\u0002rations we proof the representation (1.5) of the scattering matrix {SΘ(λ)} in\nL222@P4: Theorem 3.8. Section 4 is concerned with the spectral shift function and the\nL223@P4: Birman-Krein formula. In Section 5 we apply our general result to singular\nL224@P4: Sturm-Liouville operators with scalar and matrix potentials, to Dirac operators\nL225@P4: and to Schr¨odinger operators with point interactions. Finally, for the conve\u0002nience of the reader we repeat some basic facts on direct integrals and spectral\nL226@P4: representations in the appendix, thus making our exposition self-contained.\nL227@P4: Notations. Throughout the paper H and H denote separable Hilbert spaces\nL228@P4: with scalar product (·, ·). The linear space of bounded linear operators defined\nL229@P4: from H to H is denoted by [H, H]. For brevity we write [H] instead of [H, H].\nL230@P4: The set of closed operators in H is denoted by C(H). By Ce(H) we denote the\nL231@P4: set of closed linear relations in H. Notice that C(H) ⊆ Ce(H). The resolvent\nL232@P4: set and the spectrum of a linear operator or relation are denoted by ρ(·) and\nL233@P4: σ(·), respectively. The domain, kernel and range of a linear operator or relation\nL234@P4: are denoted by dom (·), ker(·) and ran (·), respectively. By B(R) we denote the\nL235@P4: Borel sets of R. The Lebesgue measure on B(R) is denoted by µL(·).\nL236@P4: 2 Extension theory of symmetric operators\nL237@P4: 2.1 Boundary triplets and closed extensions\nL238@P4: Let A be a densely defined closed symmetric operator with equal deficiency\nL239@P4: indices n±(A) = dim ker(A∗ ∓ i) ≤ ∞ in the separable Hilbert space H. We\nL240@P4: use the concept of boundary triplets for the description of the closed extensions\nL241@P4: AΘ ⊂ A∗ of A in H, see [12, 13, 14, 24].\nL242@P4: Definition 2.1 A triplet Π = {H, Γ0, Γ1} is called boundary triplet for the\nL243@P4: adjoint operator A∗if H is a Hilbert space and Γ0, Γ1 : dom (A∗) → H are\nL244@P4: linear mappings such that\nL245@P4: (i) the abstract second Green’s identity,\nL246@P4: (A\nL247@P4: ∗\nL248@P4: f, g) − (f, A∗g) = (Γ1f, Γ0g) − (Γ0f, Γ1g),\nL249@P4: holds for all f, g ∈ dom (A∗) and\nL250@P4-5: 5(ii) the mapping Γ := (Γ0, Γ1)\nL251@P5: ⊤ : dom (A∗\nL252@P5: ) −→ H × H is surjective.\nL253@P5: We refer to [13] and [14] for a detailed study of boundary triplets and recall\nL254@P5: only some important facts. First of all a boundary triplet Π = {H, Γ0, Γ1} for\nL255@P5: A∗exists since the deficiency indices n±(A) of A are assumed to be equal. Then\nL256@P5: n±(A) = dim H holds. We note that a boundary triplet for A∗is not unique.\nL257@P5: An operator Ae is called a proper extension of A if Ae is closed and satisfies\nL258@P5: A ⊆ Ae ⊆ A∗. Note that here A is a proper extension of itself. In order to\nL259@P5: describe the set of proper extensions of A with the help of a boundary triplet\nL260@P5: Π = {H, Γ0, Γ1} for A∗ we have to consider the set Ce(H) of closed linear relations\nL261@P5: in H, that is, the set of closed linear subspaces of H⊕H. A closed linear operator\nL262@P5: in H is identified with its graph, so that the set C(H) of closed linear operators in\nL263@P5: H is viewed as a subset of Ce(H). For the usual definitions of the linear operations\nL264@P5: with linear relations, the inverse, the resolvent set and the spectrum we refer to\nL265@P5: [15]. Recall that the adjoint relation Θ∗ ∈ Ce(H) of a linear relation Θ in H is\nL266@P5: defined as\nL267@P5: Θ\nL268@P5: ∗\nL269@P5: := \u001a\u0012k\nL270@P5: k\nL271@P5: ′\nL272@P5: \u0013\nL273@P5: : (k, h′) = (k\nL274@P5: ′\nL275@P5: , h) for all \u0012\nL276@P5: h\nL277@P5: h\nL278@P5: ′\nL279@P5: \u0013\nL280@P5: ∈ Θ\nL281@P5: \u001b\nL282@P5: (2.1)\nL283@P5: and Θ is said to be symmetric (selfadjoint) if Θ ⊆ Θ∗(resp. Θ = Θ∗). Note\nL284@P5: that definition (2.1) extends the definition of the adjoint operator.\nL285@P5: With a boundary triplet Π = {H, Γ0, Γ1} for A∗ one associates two selfad\u0002joint extensions of A defined by\nL286@P5: A0 := A\nL287@P5: ∗\nL288@P5: ↾ ker(Γ0) and A1 := A\nL289@P5: ∗\nL290@P5: ↾ ker(Γ1).\nL291@P5: A description of all proper (closed symmetric, selfadjoint) extensions of A is\nL292@P5: given in the next proposition. Note also that the selfadjointness of A0 and A1\nL293@P5: is a consequence of Proposition 2.2 (ii).\nL294@P5: Proposition 2.2 Let Π = {H, Γ0, Γ1} be a boundary triplet for A∗\nL295@P5: . Then the\nL296@P5: mapping\nL297@P5: Θ 7→ AΘ := Γ−1Θ = \bf ∈ dom (A\nL298@P5: ∗\nL299@P5: ) : Γf = (Γ0f, Γ1f)\nL300@P5: ⊤ ∈ Θ\nL301@P5: \nL302@P5: (2.2)\nL303@P5: establishes a bijective correspondence between the set Ce(H) and the set of proper\nL304@P5: extensions of A. Moreover, for Θ ∈ Ce(H) the following assertions hold.\nL305@P5: (i) (AΘ)\nL306@P5: ∗ = AΘ∗ .\nL307@P5: (ii) AΘ is symmetric (selfadjoint) if and only if Θ is symmetric (resp. selfad\u0002joint).\nL308@P5: (iii) AΘ is disjoint with A0, that is dom (AΘ) ∩ dom (A0) = dom (A), if and\nL309@P5: only if Θ ∈ C(H). In this case the extension AΘ in (2.2) is given by\nL310@P5: AΘ = A\nL311@P5: ∗\nL312@P5: ↾ kerΓ1 − ΘΓ0\nL313@P5: \u0001\nL314@P5: .\nL315@P5-6: 6In the following we shall often be concerned with simple symmetric operators.\nL316@P6: Recall that a symmetric operator is said to be simple if there is no nontrivial\nL317@P6: subspace which reduces it to a selfadjoint operator. By [26] each symmetric\nL318@P6: operator A in H can be written as the direct orthogonal sum Ab⊕ As of a simple\nL319@P6: symmetric operator Ab in the Hilbert space\nL320@P6: Hb = clospan\bker(A\nL321@P6: ∗ − λ) : λ ∈ C\\R\nL322@P6: \nL323@P6: and a selfadjoint operator As in H⊖Hb. Here clospan{·} denotes the closed linear\nL324@P6: span of a set. Obviously A is simple if and only if Hb coincides with H.\nL325@P6: 2.2 Weyl functions and resolvents of extensions\nL326@P6: Let, as in Section 2.1, A be a densely defined closed symmetric operator in H\nL327@P6: with equal deficiency indices. If λ ∈ C is a point of regular type of A, i.e.\nL328@P6: (A − λ)\nL329@P6: −1\nL330@P6: is bounded, we denote the defect subspace of A by Nλ = ker(A∗ − λ).\nL331@P6: The following definition can be found in [12, 13, 14].\nL332@P6: Definition 2.3 Let Π = {H, Γ0, Γ1} be a boundary triplet for A∗ and let A0 =\nL333@P6: A∗↾ ker(Γ0). The operator valued functions γ(·) : ρ(A0) −→ [H, H] and M(·) :\nL334@P6: ρ(A0) −→ [H] defined by\nL335@P6: γ(λ) := Γ0 ↾ Nλ\nL336@P6: \u0001−1\nL337@P6: and M(λ) := Γ1γ(λ), λ ∈ ρ(A0), (2.3)\nL338@P6: are called the γ-field and the Weyl function, respectively, corresponding to the\nL339@P6: boundary triplet Π.\nL340@P6: It follows from the identity dom (A∗) = ker(Γ0) +˙ Nλ, λ ∈ ρ(A0), where as\nL341@P6: above A0 = A∗↾ ker(Γ0), that the γ-field γ(·) in (2.3) is well defined. It is easily\nL342@P6: seen that both γ(·) and M(·) are holomorphic on ρ(A0). Moreover, the relations\nL343@P6: γ(µ) = I + (µ − λ)(A0 − µ)\nL344@P6: −1\nL345@P6: \u0001\nL346@P6: γ(λ), λ, µ ∈ ρ(A0), (2.4)\nL347@P6: and\nL348@P6: M(λ) − M(µ)\nL349@P6: ∗ = (λ − µ)γ(µ)\nL350@P6: ∗\nL351@P6: γ(λ), λ, µ ∈ ρ(A0), (2.5)\nL352@P6: are valid (see [13]). The identity (2.5) yields that M(·) is a Nevanlinna function,\nL353@P6: that is, M(·) is holomorphic on C\\R and takes values in [H], M(λ) = M(λ)\nL354@P6: ∗\nL355@P6: for all λ ∈ C\\R and ℑm (M(λ)) is a nonnegative operator for all λ in the upper\nL356@P6: half plane C+ = {λ ∈ C : ℑm λ > 0}. Moreover, it follows from (2.5) that\nL357@P6: 0 ∈ ρ(ℑm (M(λ))) holds. It is important to note that if the operator A is\nL358@P6: simple, then the Weyl function M(·) determines the pair {A, A0} uniquely up\nL359@P6: to unitary equivalence, cf. [12, 13].\nL360@P6: In the case that the deficiency indices n+(A) = n−(A) are finite the Weyl\nL361@P6: function M corresponding to Π = {H, Γ0, Γ1} is a matrix-valued Nevanlinna\nL362@P6: function in the finite dimensional space H. From [16, 18] one gets the existence\nL363@P6: of the (strong) limit\nL364@P6: M(λ + i0) = lim\nL365@P6: ǫ→+0\nL366@P6: M(λ + iǫ)\nL367@P6-7: 7from the upper half-plane for a.e. λ ∈ R.\nL368@P7: Let now Π = {H, Γ0, Γ1} be a boundary triplet for A∗ with